.TH INTRO 3
.SH NAME
intro \- introduction to library functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B #include <math.h>
.SH DESCRIPTION
This section describes functions that may be found
in various libraries, other than those functions
that directly invoke UNIX system primitives,
which are described in section 2.
Functions are divided into various libraries distinguished
by the section number at the top of the page:
.TP 6n
(3)
These functions, together with those of section 2 and those
marked (3S), constitute library
.I libc,
which is automatically loaded by the C compiler
.IR b6cc (1).
The link editor
.IR b6ld (1)
searches this library under the `\-lc' option.
Declarations for some of these functions may be obtained
from include files indicated on the appropriate pages.
.B Note:
there is no Fortran compiler here.
.TP
(3M)
These functions constitute the math library,
.I libm.
The link editor searches this library under the `\-lm' option.
Declarations for these functions may be obtained from
the include file <math.h>.
.B Note:
its pages live in lib/libm, not here.
.TP
(3S)
These functions constitute the
`standard I/O package',
see
.IR stdio (3).
These functions are in the library
.I libc
already mentioned.
Declarations for these functions may be obtained from
the include file <stdio.h>.
.TP
(3X)
Various
specialized libraries have not been given distinctive
captions.
The files in which these libraries are found are named
on the appropriate pages.
.B Note:
the two that exist here are
.I libtermcap
and
.IR libcurses ,
whose pages live in lib/libtermcap and lib/libcurses.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
share/besm6/lib/libm.a, libtermcap.a, libcurses.a
.br
share/besm6/lib/crt0.o, libruntime.a
.SH SEE ALSO
stdio(3),
b6nm(1), b6ld(1), b6cc(1), intro(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Functions in the math library (3M) may return
conventional values when the function is undefined for the
given arguments or when the value is not representable.
In these cases the external variable
.I errno
(see
.IR intro (2))
is set to the value EDOM
or ERANGE.
.B Note:
EDOM and ERANGE are defined in <errno.h>, not in <math.h> as v7's page said.
.PP
.B Note:
"when the value is not representable" is sharper here than it was on the PDP-11.
This machine has
.I no infinities and no NaNs,
so a routine that would have returned one returns HUGE_VAL \- the largest
.I finite
value, and not a distinguished one.  And an exponent that overflows 2**63 is an
arithmetic
.I fault
that ends the program, so every range check in libm is made
.I before
the arithmetic rather than after it.  See exp(3M).
.SH ASSEMBLER
v7's page gave a PDP-11 calling sequence here.  The convention on this machine is
in doc/Besm6_Calling_Conventions.md: arguments are pushed in direct order below
r15, the
.I last
one is left in the accumulator, r14 holds the negative argument count, r13 the
return address, and the result comes back in the accumulator.
.PP
.B Note:
one consequence shows up on several pages in section 2.  A system-call stub has
.I no prologue and does not pop the stack,
because the C convention has already put the arguments where the gate reads them
and the gate performs the callee's cleanup itself.  That is why lib/libc/sys is
assembly and not C.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS Link order is a contract
.PP
.RS
.nf
crt0.o objects... [\-lm] [\-lcurses] [\-ltermcap] \-lc \-lruntime
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I B6ld
scans each archive exactly once, where it stands, and pulls a member only for a
symbol still undefined.  libm calls into libc, libcurses calls into libtermcap
and libc, libtermcap calls into libc, libc calls the compiler-support helpers of
libruntime, and
.B none of them calls back.
.PP
.I Libruntime.a
comes from the external c-compiler and supplies nothing but those helpers.  The
libc, the crt0.o and the headers are this repository's.
.SS One hazard is silent
A routine missing from libc.a can be satisfied from libruntime.a instead of
failing to link.  Relink without it and read the errors \- every undefined name
must be a
.BR b$* :
.PP
.RS
.nf
b6ld crt0.o prog.o \-o /dev/null \-L.../libc \-lc
.fi
.RE
